{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The thoracic duct is the largest lymphatic vessel in the body, about 45 cm long. It begins at the cisterna chyli near the twelfth thoracic vertebra, passes through the aortic opening of the diaphragm, crosses from right to left at T5, arches above the clavicle, and opens at the left subclavian and left internal jugular vein junction.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN23.7" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## What is the thoracic duct?

**Lymph** is the tissue fluid that the lymphatic vessels collect and return to the blood. The lymphatic system is essentially a drainage system, not a circulation. Almost all lymph in the body eventually passes into the thorax and re-enters the veins through one of two channels: the **thoracic duct** or the **right lymphatic duct**.

The thoracic duct extends from the upper part of the abdomen to the lower part of the neck, crossing the posterior and superior parts of the mediastinum (the central compartment of the thorax). It is about 45 cm long. It looks beaded because there are many valves in its lumen. These valves let lymph flow only towards the heart.

## Which regions does it drain?

The thoracic duct carries lymph from:

- Both lower limbs
- The pelvic cavity
- The abdominal cavity
- The left side of the thorax
- The left upper limb
- The left side of the head and neck

So it drains both halves of the body below the diaphragm and the left half above it. The **right lymphatic duct** drains the rest: the right side of the head and neck, the right upper limb and the right side of the thorax.

Some organs drain lymph straight into the thoracic duct without passing through lymph nodes first. These include the thyroid gland, the oesophagus, and the coronary and triangular ligaments of the liver.

## Origin: the cisterna chyli

The duct begins as the upward continuation of the **cisterna chyli**, a saccular or spindle-shaped dilation of the lymphatics. The cisterna chyli is formed most commonly by the union of the intestinal lymph trunk and the lumbar lymph trunks. It usually lies in front of the first and second lumbar vertebrae, behind the right crus of the diaphragm and to the right of the abdominal aorta.

The duct itself usually begins to the right of the midline, around the lower border of the twelfth thoracic vertebra. Descriptions of the exact level differ between sources. Both the cisterna chyli and the duct vary in origin, course and length, and the cisterna chyli varies even more in width and shape.

On CT the cisterna chyli can often be recognised. The thoracic duct itself is smaller and is not normally seen on CT, though it may be opacified after lymphography (an X-ray study of the lymphatic vessels using contrast).

## Course

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/thoracic-duct/thoracic-duct-course.webp" alt="Diagram of the thoracic duct running from the abdomen to the neck." caption="The thoracic duct ascends from L1 to the root of the neck and opens at the left venous angle." credit="SGUL lymres, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anatomy_of_the_lymphatic_system.jpg" width="960" height="1387" /%}

{% table %}
- **Part**
- **Level**
- **What happens**
---
- **Abdominal origin**
- Around T12 to L2
- The cisterna chyli continues upward as the duct
---
- **Entry to thorax**
- Aortic opening of the diaphragm
- The duct passes through the aortic hiatus, not the vena caval opening
---
- **Posterior mediastinum**
- T12 up to T5
- Lies to the right, between the aorta and the azygos vein
---
- **Crossing over**
- At about T5
- Crosses the vertebral column from right to left, behind the oesophagus
---
- **Superior mediastinum**
- Above T5
- Runs up along the left edge of the oesophagus, behind the aortic arch and behind the left subclavian artery
---
- **Neck**
- Level of the transverse process of C7
- Arches laterally about 3 to 4 cm above the clavicle, then descends in front of the first part of the left subclavian artery
---
- **Termination**
- Root of the neck
- Opens at the angle between the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins
{% /table %}

In the retrocrural space (between the diaphragmatic crura and the vertebral bodies) the duct travels with the aorta, the azygos venous system, the intercostal arteries, the sympathetic chains and the splanchnic nerves.

## Calibre and flow

When present as a single channel, the duct is about 5 mm wide at its origin. It narrows to 2 to 3 mm at mid-thoracic level and widens again at its termination. It is sinuous and has valves throughout its length. A bicuspid valve at its termination helps prevent blood from flowing back into the duct.

Total lymph flow through the duct is 1500 to 2400 ml per day. It rises and falls with the amount of fat in the diet, especially long-chain triglycerides.

## Relations of the cervical arch

The arch in the neck lies in the space between longus colli, the anterior scalene muscle and the dome of the pleura. Know these relations:

- The arch is related to the **left common carotid artery, left vagus nerve and left internal jugular vein**.
- It crosses **in front of** the vertebral artery and vein, the left sympathetic trunk, the vertebral ganglion, the thyrocervical trunk and its branches, and the left phrenic nerve.
- It lies against the medial border of scalenus anterior.
- The prevertebral fascia covers these structures.
- It descends in front of the first part of the left subclavian artery.

The terminal parts of the thoracic duct and of the right lymphatic trunk are hidden by the **scalene fat pad**. This makes them more vulnerable during neck surgery.

## Tributaries

{% table %}
- **Tributary**
- **Notes**
---
- **Cisterna chyli**
- Receives almost all lymph from below the diaphragm, through the intestinal and lumbar trunks
---
- **Ascending lumbar trunks**
- Pierce the diaphragmatic crus on each side and join the duct in the posterior mediastinum
---
- **Intercostal lymph trunks**
- The lower six or seven descend from the intercostal nodes and end in the duct, directly or by common trunks
---
- **Posterior mediastinal nodes**
- Send lymph vessels to the duct
---
- **Mediastinal trunks**
- Drain the convex (upper) surface of the liver, the diaphragm, pericardium, heart and oesophagus; many have terminal bicuspid valves
---
- **Left jugular trunk**
- Drains the left head and neck; usually joins the duct but may open into the left internal jugular vein
---
- **Left subclavian trunk**
- Drains the left upper limb from the axillary nodes; usually joins the duct but may open directly into the left subclavian vein
---
- **Left bronchomediastinal trunk**
- Drains the left half of the thorax; some sources say it ends in the duct, others say it usually has its own venous opening
{% /table %}

The duct also receives lymph from the lung tissue and the parietal pleura (the lining of the chest wall). Some tributaries may open directly into a vein instead of the duct.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Memory line:** the duct starts low (around T12), crosses at T5 from right to left, and finishes high on the left side of the neck. Lymph from the right side of the head, neck, thorax and right arm goes elsewhere, to the right lymphatic duct.
{% /callout %}

## Right lymphatic duct (AN23.7)

The right jugular, right subclavian and right bronchomediastinal lymph trunks may join to form the **right lymphatic duct**. These drain the right side of the head and neck, the right upper limb and the right side of the thorax. If present, this common duct is only about 1.3 cm long. It opens into the beginning of the right brachiocephalic vein, at the junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins.

In some people the three trunks open separately into the great veins at the root of the neck. In up to one-fifth of individuals they join to form a right lymphatic trunk about 0.5 to 1 cm long.

## Variations

The classical description above is found in only about 50 to 65 percent of people. Variations are common:

- The duct may ascend as **multiple channels** or as a plexus instead of a single duct.
- It may divide into two or three branches that reunite or drain into both left and right venous systems. In some people two ducts exist in the mediastinum.
- A single duct may empty into right-sided veins. This is more frequent when the right subclavian artery is aberrant (it arises from the distal aortic arch and ascends behind the oesophagus). In a small proportion of cases the duct terminates on the right side (6 percent).
- On the left, a systematic review found the duct most often enters the **internal jugular vein** (46 percent), then the **junction of the internal jugular and subclavian veins** (32 percent), then the **subclavian vein** (18 percent). This differs from the traditional teaching of a junction opening.

## Chyle and fat absorption

The lymphatic vessels of the small intestine begin as **lacteals**, blind-ending projections inside each villus. After a fatty meal the lymph in them looks milky white because it carries fat particles called **chylomicrons**. This milky lymph is called **chyle**.

Chyle carries cholesterol, long-chain fatty acids, triglycerides and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K straight into the circulation, bypassing the liver. This is why the thoracic duct carries more fat-rich lymph after meals.

Lymphatic vessels are found in nearly all tissues. They are absent from cartilage, bone, the central nervous system, the eyeball, the internal ear and the epidermis of the skin.

## Development

Lymph sacs appear in the embryo at 6 to 7 weeks of gestation. They enlarge and connect so that lymph from the lower limbs and abdomen drains through the cisterna chyli into the thoracic duct. The duct in turn drains into the left internal jugular vein at its junction with the left subclavian vein.

## Applied anatomy: injury and chylothorax

**Chylothorax** is the collection of lymphatic fluid (chyle) in the pleural space. It follows injury or disease of the thoracic duct or its main tributaries. The anatomy of the duct decides where the effusion forms.

- **Suspect it** in penetrating trauma near the left proximal subclavian vein. The diagnosis is rarely made in the emergency department.
- **Consequences:** metabolic, nutritional and immune upset. In trauma the quoted mortality is 50 percent, so early recognition matters.
- **Diagnosis:** pleural fluid is tested for triglyceride content, and lipid electrophoresis shows chylomicrons. Lymphangiography can locate a lymphatic leak.
- **Chylous ascites** (chyle in the peritoneal cavity) is confirmed in the same way, by finding chylomicrons in the aspirated fluid.

{% table %}
- **Approach**
- **Examples and key points**
---
- **Conservative**
- Observation, treating the underlying disease, a strict medium-chain triglyceride diet or total parenteral nutrition (feeding into a vein), thoracocentesis (draining the pleural fluid with a needle), tube thoracostomy with chemical pleurodesis
---
- **Why a medium-chain triglyceride diet?**
- Medium-chain triglycerides are absorbed directly into the blood and not via the lymphatics, so lymph flow falls
---
- **Minimal-access**
- Thoracic duct embolization (blocking the duct) after lymphangiography has a success rate above 50 percent in expert hands. Needle disruption of the duct or cisterna chyli has a clinical success rate of 60.8 percent. The overall complication rate is low, about 0 to 2 percent, with no reported mortality.
---
- **Surgical**
- Pleurectomy, talc poudrage, pleuroperitoneal shunting, or repair or ligation of the duct
---
- **Cancer-related chylothorax**
- Pleurodesis (closing off the pleural space) is highly successful in preventing recurrence when drainage persists despite chemotherapy and radiotherapy
{% /table %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The thoracic duct leaves the abdomen through the aortic opening (aortic hiatus) of the diaphragm, lying beside the aorta. It does not pass through the vena caval opening." %}
The thoracic duct enters the thorax through the:
- A. Vena caval opening of the diaphragm
- B. Oesophageal opening of the diaphragm
- C. Aortic opening of the diaphragm
- D. Sternocostal gap of the diaphragm
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The duct ascends on the right side in the posterior mediastinum and crosses over to the left at about the level of the fifth thoracic vertebra, behind the oesophagus." %}
The thoracic duct crosses from the right side to the left side at the level of:
- A. Twelfth thoracic vertebra
- B. Fifth thoracic vertebra
- C. Seventh cervical vertebra
- D. Second lumbar vertebra
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="The right upper limb drains by the right subclavian trunk into the right lymphatic duct. The thoracic duct drains both lower limbs, the abdomen, the left side of the thorax and the left upper limb and left head and neck." %}
Lymph from which region does NOT normally drain into the thoracic duct?
- A. Left upper limb
- B. Both lower limbs
- C. Left side of the thorax
- D. Right upper limb
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The duct terminates at the angle where the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins join. Variations exist, but this is the classical termination." %}
The classical termination of the thoracic duct is at the junction of the:
- A. Left subclavian and left internal jugular veins
- B. Right subclavian and right internal jugular veins
- C. Left brachiocephalic vein and superior vena cava
- D. Azygos vein and superior vena cava
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Medium-chain triglycerides are absorbed directly into the blood and not through the lymphatics. They therefore reduce chyle flow through the thoracic duct, while long-chain fats raise it." %}
A patient with chylothorax is advised a diet rich in medium-chain triglycerides. The anatomical reason is that these fats:
- A. Are carried in lacteals as chylomicrons
- B. Are absorbed directly into the blood rather than the lymphatics
- C. Are absorbed only in the large intestine
- D. Are carried to the liver by the thoracic duct
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The cisterna chyli is usually formed by the union of the intestinal lymph trunk and the lumbar lymph trunks. It then continues upward as the thoracic duct." %}
The cisterna chyli is usually formed by the union of:
- A. Right and left bronchomediastinal trunks
- B. Jugular and subclavian trunks
- C. The intestinal lymph trunk and the lumbar lymph trunks
- D. The intercostal and mediastinal lymph trunks
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What is the thoracic duct and how long is it?" %}
It is the largest lymphatic vessel in the body, about 45 cm long. It runs from the upper abdomen to the lower neck and has a beaded appearance because of its many valves.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Where does it begin?" %}
As the upward continuation of the cisterna chyli, near the lower border of the twelfth thoracic vertebra. The cisterna chyli lies in front of the first and second lumbar vertebrae.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the course of the thoracic duct." %}
It passes through the aortic opening of the diaphragm into the posterior mediastinum, ascends between the aorta and the azygos vein, and crosses from right to left at about T5. It runs along the left edge of the oesophagus in the superior mediastinum, arches about 3 to 4 cm above the clavicle at the level of C7, and descends in front of the first part of the left subclavian artery to end at the left subclavian and internal jugular junction.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which regions are drained by the thoracic duct and which by the right lymphatic duct?" %}
The thoracic duct drains both lower limbs, pelvis, abdomen, the left side of the thorax and the left upper limb and left head and neck. The right lymphatic duct drains the right side of the head and neck, the right upper limb and the right side of the thorax.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the tributaries of the thoracic duct." %}
The cisterna chyli, the ascending lumbar trunks, the lower intercostal lymph trunks, mediastinal trunks and posterior mediastinal nodes, and, at the root of the neck, the left jugular, left subclavian and left bronchomediastinal trunks.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is chylothorax and when do you suspect it?" %}
It is a collection of chyle in the pleural space after injury or obstruction of the thoracic duct or its tributaries. Suspect it after penetrating trauma near the left proximal subclavian vein.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why does the thoracic duct vary so much, and why does it matter?" %}
The classical pattern is found in only about 50 to 65 percent of people. The duct may be double or plexiform, or may end in right-sided veins. A surgeon or radiologist cannot assume a single standard duct.
{% /viva %}

